    rdfs:comment "Global Strategist, Rabobank Singapore, analyzing geopolitics/economics/markets intersections. 25+ years as economist/strategist across roles at Silk Road Associates (Bangkok), Royal Bank of Canada (London/Sydney), and Dun & Bradstreet (London); has lived and worked in 9-10 countries. Invited presenter, 2022 G-20. BA, Lancaster University; master's, University College London. Real, extensive prior TGS history: Reality Roundtable #16, Reality Roundtable #12, and Episode #118 ('The Many-Isms of the Metacrisis'), all now built. No independent Wikipedia/DBpedia page found — confirmed real via Rabobank's own staff page and extensive independent financial-media coverage. Real, published intellectual position worth noting directly: wrote an extensive rebuttal of tgs:Work.BrettonWoodsIII (Zoltan Pozsar's real, popularized theory of a coming commodity-backed multipolar monetary order), arguing the theory reads more as a compelling narrative than a real structural shift — his own real, core points (per a real secondary source summarizing his rebuttal, since the original is paywalled): governments won't readily give up unbacked fiat's convenience for a commodity standard; rival currencies lack the US dollar's trust and scale, and the yuan itself remains soft-pegged to the dollar; most mineral, food, and energy exporters aren't clearly aligned with either geopolitical bloc; and commodities trade represents only roughly a quarter of global trade, smaller a share than the theory's framing suggests."@en ;
    .

