tgs:Human.EdConway a thinkr:Human, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "Ed Conway"@en ;
    rdfs:label "Ed Conway (human)"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Economics and Data Editor at Sky News (UK), author of Material World and two other books, educated at Oxford and Harvard. Governor of the National Institute for Economic and Social Research (UK). Lectures on the international monetary system at LSE and the US Treasury."@en ;
    owl:sameAs <http://dbpedia.org/resource/Ed_Conway> ;
    .

tgs:Human.StacyMitchell a thinkr:Human, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "Stacy Mitchell"@en ;
    rdfs:label "Stacy Mitchell (human)"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "American activist, writer, and author. Grew up in Portland, Maine; B.A. in history, Macalester College. Joined the Institute for Local Self-Reliance as a research assistant in 1997, now Co-Executive Director, leading its Independent Business Initiative. Chair of the American Independent Business Alliance. Author of 'Big-Box Swindle: The True Cost of Mega-Retailers and the Fight for America's Independent Businesses' (Beacon Press, 2007); co-author of the 2016 report 'Amazon's Stranglehold.' Her research was cited in Congress's 2021 investigation of Big Tech dominance and informed the FTC's 2023 antitrust lawsuit against Amazon. Named one of the top ten 'antitrust super influencers' by political strategy firm Baron, 2022. Co-founded the Athena coalition and Small Business Rising. Also serves on the board of the Maine Center for Economic Policy."@en ;
    owl:sameAs <https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stacy_Mitchell> ;
    thinkr:memberOf tgs:Organization.ILSR .

tgs:Human.GregElliott a thinkr:Human, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "Greg Elliott"@en ;
    rdfs:label "Greg Elliott (human)"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Systems theorist and independent researcher at the intersection of game theory, evolutionary biology, and institutional analysis. Siemens solutions consultant specializing in data science and AI; co-founder of Orbyfy. Author of tgs:Work.PsychopathicSelectionHypothesisBook — argues cooperation is evolutionarily stable in small tribal systems, but modern systems (anonymity, abstraction, scale) make exploitative/instrumental strategies more profitable, producing 'entrapment games' where pro-social people become behaviorally locked into relationships, jobs, and systems where leaving costs more than staying. Founder, tgs:Organization.InstituteForSymbolicSelection. Prior career in financial markets — real, direct personal account of observing behavioral profiles well-adapted to that competitive environment, which he says helped seed the theory. TGS guest, Episode 231, 'The Psychopathic Selection Hypothesis: The Rules of the Game Keep Us Trapped.' No owl:sameAs found — not independently verified as notable enough for a dedicated Wikipedia/DBpedia page."@en ;
    thinkr:memberOf tgs:Organization.InstituteForSymbolicSelection ;
    .

tgs:Human.JohnGowdy a thinkr:Human, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "John Gowdy"@en ;
    rdfs:label "John Gowdy (human)"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "John M. Gowdy, Professor of Economics and Professor of Science and Technology Studies, Rensselaer Polytechnic Institute (Troy, NY). Ecological economist; recipient of the Herman Daly Award for contributions to ecological economics. Author of tgs:Work.Ultrasocial2021 ('Ultrasocial: The Evolution of Human Nature and the Quest for a Sustainable Future,' Cambridge University Press, Oct 2021) and 'Limited Wants, Unlimited Means: A Reader on Hunter-Gatherer Economics and the Environment.' Co-authored research on ants and social insects with Lisi Krall (SUNY Cortland, not yet built in this graph). Real, direct UN conservation work on South Sudan's Sudd wetland (second-largest wetland in the world after the Pantanal), threatened by planned drainage for downstream agriculture. Nate's own real, direct words describe him as 'my friend and one of my intellectual mentors' with a prior relationship spanning roughly 20 years — the deepest pre-existing relationship of any guest built in this graph to date. TGS guest, Episode 14, 'Superorganisms, Crazy Ants, and Fire Apes, Oh My!' — explores capitalism, economic surplus, and evolutionary mechanisms in relation to the economic Superorganism, and how much real agency individuals retain against it."@en ;
    .

tgs:Human.LynAlden a thinkr:Human, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "Lyn Alden"@en ;
    rdfs:label "Lyn Alden (human)"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Independent investment analyst, founder of Lyn Alden Investment Strategy. Background in electrical engineering (BS) and engineering management (MS, focus on engineering economics, systems engineering, financial modeling); worked over a decade as an electrical engineer at the FAA's William J. Hughes Technical Center before moving into finance. Independent director, Swan.com; general partner, Ego Death Capital. Author of tgs:Work.BrokenMoney2023 (2023 bestseller)."@en ;
    .

tgs:Human.TristanHarris a thinkr:Human, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "Tristan Harris"@en ;
    rdfs:label "Tristan Harris (human)"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Co-Founder and President, Center for Humane Technology (CHT), a nonprofit aligning technology with humanity's best interests. Co-hosts the podcast 'Your Undivided Attention' with Aza Raskin and Daniel Barclay. Formerly Design Ethicist at Google. Primary subject of the Netflix documentary 'The Social Dilemma' (2020, Emmy-winning, reaching over 100 million people in 190 countries), which exposed social media's psychological engineering of user behavior. Named to Time 100 Next and Rolling Stone's '25 People Shaping the World.' Featured in the 2026 documentary 'The AI Doc: Or How I Became an Apocaloptimist.' Regularly briefs heads of state, tech CEOs, and US Congress members on AI safety and social media harms."@en ;
    thinkr:memberOf tgs:Organization.CenterForHumaneTechnology ;
    .

tgs:Human.JohnVervaeke a thinkr:Human, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "John Vervaeke"@en ;
    rdfs:label "John Vervaeke (human)"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Associate professor of psychology and cognitive science, University of Toronto; director of the Cognitive Science program and the Consciousness and Wisdom Studies Laboratory. Researches intelligence, rationality, wisdom, and meaning in life, emphasizing relevance realization and non-propositional knowing. Co-author of tgs:Work.ZombiesInWesternCulture with Christopher Mastropietro (not yet built in this graph). Creator of the widely-viewed YouTube series 'Awakening from the Meaning Crisis.' Left a fundamentalist Christian upbringing he describes as personally traumatizing, later returning to Socratic/Neoplatonic philosophy alongside Tai Chi Chuan and Vipassana meditation practice — a real, direct personal origin for his own academic focus on the meaning crisis."@en ;
    .

tgs:Human.SteveVavrus a thinkr:Human, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "Steve Vavrus"@en ;
    rdfs:label "Steve Vavrus (human)"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Senior Scientist, Nelson Institute Center for Climatic Research, University of Wisconsin-Madison. Uses computer climate models and observational data to study Arctic climate dynamics and extreme weather. Co-director, Wisconsin Initiative on Climate Change Impacts (WICCI); long-time member of its Climate Working Group. IPCC scientist, contributed to the organization's most recent climate report. PhD and MS in meteorology, University of Wisconsin; BS in meteorology, Purdue University."@en ;
    .

tgs:Human.TaylorGuthrie a thinkr:Human, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "Taylor Guthrie"@en ;
    rdfs:label "Taylor Guthrie (human)"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "PhD, social cognitive neuroscientist. Doctorate from the University of Oregon, where his research focused on the default mode network and neural mechanisms underlying identity, motivation, and social cognition; received the university's Distinguished Teaching Award. Creates public-facing content including 'The Cellular Republic,' a lecture series demystifying cognitive and social neuroscience. At the time of his TGS appearance, preparing to move to the Netherlands for a postdoctoral fellowship."@en ;
    .

tgs:Human.BrettKencairn a thinkr:Human, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "Brett KenCairn"@en ;
    rdfs:label "Brett KenCairn (human)"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Founding Director, Center for Regenerative Solutions; Senior Policy Advisor for Climate and Resilience, City of Boulder's Climate Initiatives Team, where he coordinates the city's nature-based solutions work (roughly 15 years with the city). Co-founder of the Rogue River Institute for Ecology and Economy, Veterans Green Jobs, and Community Energy Systems. Real, direct professional shift documented in his own TGS appearance: began in conventional carbon-accounting climate work before real, unexplained discrepancies in the numbers led him toward the view that climate is fundamentally a biologically-mediated dynamic, not simply a carbon-in/carbon-out machine."@en ;
    .

tgs:Human.BayoAkomolafe a thinkr:Human, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "Bayo Akomolafe"@en ;
    rdfs:label "Bayo Akomolafe (human)"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Philosopher, writer, activist, professor of psychology; Visionary Founder and Executive Director, The Emergence Network. Born 1983 in western Nigeria to Yoruba parents; his family emigrated to Bonn, Germany following his father's diplomatic posting. Lost his father to a sudden heart complication in Kinshasa, Zaire during his childhood — a formative loss that shaped his later work on trauma, healing, and decolonized ways of knowing. PhD; former Special Envoy, International Alliance for Localization (2014); has taught at Middlebury College, Sonoma State University, Simon Fraser University, Schumacher College, Pacifica Graduate Institute, and University of Vermont. Originated the concepts of 'postactivism,' 'transraciality,' and 'ontofugitivity.' Author of tgs:Work.TheseWildsBeyondOurFences and 'We Will Tell Our Own Story: The Lions of Africa Speak.' Recipient, 2021 New Thought Walden Award. As of August 2025, Hubert Humphrey Distinguished Professor of American Studies, Macalester College (Saint Paul, Minnesota) — a more current position than his own official bio page (dated 2022) reflects. Real, direct connection to this graph: cited in Frankly_140 (his own real essay, 'A Slower Urgency') for the phrase 'the world is urgent, we have to move slow.'"@en ;
    .

tgs:Human.JeanMarcJancovici a thinkr:Human, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "Jean-Marc Jancovici"@en ;
    rdfs:label "Jean-Marc Jancovici (human)"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "French engineering consultant, professor, author, and independent columnist; one of France's most prominent public voices on energy, climate, and economics. Co-founder, Carbone 4 consultancy (low-carbon transition, biodiversity impacts, climate risk). Founding president, The Shift Project, a Paris-based think tank. Associate professor, Mines ParisTech; member, French High Council for the Climate. Graduate, École polytechnique and Télécom ParisTech. Author or co-author of 8 books, including tgs:Work.LePlanDeTransformation (100,000+ copies) and tgs:Work.LeMondeSansFin (a graphic novel with Christophe Blain, over 500,000 copies, France's best-selling book of 2022). Real, distinctive contribution to this graph's own vocabulary: the concept of 'sobriété' — deliberately choosing to forgo a good or service to conserve resources, versus poverty (the identical physical outcome, but unchosen)."@en ;
    .

tgs:Human.KrisDeDecker a thinkr:Human, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "Kris De Decker"@en ;
    rdfs:label "Kris De Decker (human)"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Shifted from a journalism career covering high technology to researching and writing about low technology. Creator and author of Low-Tech Magazine (solar.lowtechmagazine.com, itself solar-powered and intermittently offline as a demonstration of its own thesis) and No Tech Magazine. Creator of the Human Power Plant, a real Netherlands-based art/research project envisioning a neighborhood run substantially on human-generated power. Contributed articles to Mother Earth News, Techniques et Culture, The Oil Drum, Resilience, and several Dutch/Belgian publications. Author of 'Energie in 2030' (advised the Dutch government on science and technology challenges) and 'Stralingswarmte: gezonde warmte met minder energie' (a guide to radiant heating). Based in Barcelona, Spain, where he runs a real, personal hybrid solar/human-power system in his own apartment."@en ;
    .

tgs:Human.LukeKemp a thinkr:Human, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "Luke Kemp"@en ;
    rdfs:label "Luke Kemp (human)"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Research Affiliate, Centre for the Study of Existential Risk (CSER) and Darwin College, University of Cambridge. Research focuses on the history and future of extreme global risk. Has advised the WHO and other international institutions; work covered by the BBC, New York Times, and The New Yorker. PhD in International Relations and BA (Interdisciplinary Studies, first class honours), Australian National University. Author of tgs:Work.GoliathsCurse (Penguin, 2025), the product of roughly seven years of research."@en ;
    .

tgs:Human.HelenThompson a thinkr:Human, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "Helen Thompson"@en ;
    rdfs:label "Helen Thompson (human)"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Professor of Political Economy, Cambridge University. Research focuses on the political economy of energy and the long history behind the democratic, economic, and geopolitical disruptions of the 21st century. Regular panelist, Talking Politics; columnist, New Statesman; co-presenter, UnHerd's podcast 'These Times.' Author of tgs:Work.Disorder2022 and tgs:Work.OilAndTheWesternEconomicCrisis. Real, direct personal origin for her energy focus: born 1967 in Britain; her first memory of national life is the winter of 1974, age seven, her mother cooking by candlelight during the UK's real, widespread power cuts — a formative, visceral early sense of energy's direct impact on daily life, though she didn't connect it to her scholarship until around 2013."@en ;
    .

tgs:Human.ChrisKeefer a thinkr:Human, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "Chris Keefer"@en ;
    rdfs:label "Chris Keefer (human)"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "MD, CCFP-EM; practicing emergency physician and medical instructor in Toronto. Host of the Decouple podcast (energy, climate, environment, politics, philosophy). Founder and president, Canadians for Nuclear Energy (grassroots non-profit); Director, Doctors for Nuclear Energy. Self-described as having gone 'from being an idealist to a realist' over the two years between his two real TGS appearances, and jokingly calls himself a 'nuclear Mormon' for his advocacy work. Real, direct personal detail: half Ukrainian, describes himself as having a clinically-diagnosed tendency toward 'defensive pessimism,' which he ties directly to Ukrainian national history and identity."@en ;
    .

tgs:Human.JaneMuncke a thinkr:Human, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "Jane Muncke"@en ;
    rdfs:label "Jane Muncke (human)"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "PhD, environmental toxicology, and MSc, environmental science, ETH Zurich. Since 2012, Managing Director and Chief Scientific Officer, Food Packaging Forum Foundation (FPF), Zurich — a charitable research and science-communication organization focused on chemicals in food contact materials. Full scientific member, Society of Toxicology, Society for Environmental Chemistry and Toxicology, American Chemical Society, and the Endocrine Society. Since 2019, elected expert member, Bio Suisse's committee on trade and processing (organic food packaging standards). Director, the FAN initiative (resource overshoot, polycrisis, societal collapse). Participated as an independent scientist in the UN's plastics-treaty negotiating process (INC2 through INC5)."@en ;
    .

tgs:Human.PeterBrannen a thinkr:Human, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "Peter Brannen"@en ;
    rdfs:label "Peter Brannen (human)"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Award-winning science journalist and contributing writer, The Atlantic; also published in The New York Times, The Washington Post, Wired, Aeon, The Boston Globe, Slate, and The Guardian. Author of tgs:Work.TheEndsOfTheWorld and tgs:Work.TheStoryOfCO2. 2023 visiting scholar, Kluge Center, Library of Congress; affiliate, Institute of Arctic and Alpine Research, University of Colorado-Boulder; formerly a 2018 Scripps Fellow (CU-Boulder), 2015 journalist-in-residence at the National Evolutionary Synthesis Center (Duke), and 2011 Ocean Science Journalism Fellow, Woods Hole Oceanographic Institution."@en ;
    .

tgs:Human.SamanthaSweetwater a thinkr:Human, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "Samantha Sweetwater"@en ;
    rdfs:label "Samantha Sweetwater (human)"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Wisdom guide, author, and founder of One Life Circle ('a ministry of remembering'). Over three decades facilitating individuals and organizations across five continents through personal, cultural, ecological, and spiritual emergence; mentors leaders in business, technology, and finance on navigating awakening and aligning impact with regenerative futures. Founder, Dancing Freedom and Peacebody Japan (embodied-awakening movements; has trained hundreds of facilitators). Former seed farmer. MA, Wisdom Studies; BA, Social Theory and Dance. Initiated into indigenous lineages of Africa (the Bwiti tradition, Gabon), Latin America, and Turtle Island. Author of tgs:Work.TrueHuman."@en ;
    .

tgs:Human.TadPatzek a thinkr:Human, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "Tad Patzek"@en ;
    rdfs:label "Tad Patzek (human)"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Professor Emeritus of Petroleum and Chemical Engineering, Earth Sciences Division, and Director of the Ali I. Al-Naimi Petroleum Engineering Research Center, KAUST (Saudi Arabia). Formerly the Lois K. and Richard D. Folger Leadership Professor and Chairman, Petroleum and Geosystems Engineering Department, University of Texas at Austin; formerly Professor of Geoengineering, UC Berkeley; formerly a researcher at Shell Development, a company managed for 20 years by M. King Hubbert. Full Presidential Professor in Poland (the country's highest academic honor). Served on the DOI Macondo Well Advisory Committee. Research focuses on thermodynamics and ecology of human survival, and food/energy supply for humanity. Co-author of over 400 papers and reports; author of tgs:Work.ThermalPowerAndClimateChange. Real, exceptionally long personal history with Nate — Nate's own words: 'I've known you for going on 20 years,' the longest documented pre-existing relationship of any guest in this graph."@en ;
    .

tgs:Human.DavidSloanWilson a thinkr:Human, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "David Sloan Wilson"@en ;
    rdfs:label "David Sloan Wilson (human)"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "SUNY Distinguished Professor of Biology and Anthropology Emeritus, Binghamton University. President, ProSocial World. Co-authored (with E.O. Wilson) the real, foundational 2007 paper 'Rethinking the Theoretical Foundation of Sociobiology,' resurrecting group selection as multi-level selection theory after decades of it being considered scientifically taboo; his own first paper on the topic dates to 1975. Author of Evolution for Everyone (2007), Darwin's Cathedral, This View of Life: Completing the Darwinian Revolution, Prosocial: Using Evolutionary Science to Build Productive, Equitable, and Collaborative Groups (with Paul Atkins and Steven C. Hayes), and a novel, Atlas Hugged: The Autobiography of John Galt III. Real, direct personal connection to Nate: Charles Goodnight — Wilson's own real, close collaborator and friend, since deceased — served on Nate's PhD committee in ecological economics, an actual academic-lineage link between the two independent of this episode itself. Son of novelist Sloan Wilson (The Man in the Gray Flannel Suit)."@en ;
    .

tgs:Human.CharlesGoodnight a thinkr:Human, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "Charles Goodnight"@en ;
    rdfs:label "Charles Goodnight (human)"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Professor of Biology, University of Vermont (1955-2020); joined UVM in 1988 as Assistant Professor, promoted to Associate Professor 1994, Full Professor 1999; recognized as a University Scholar 2002-2003 for sustained research excellence. PhD, University of Chicago, during a real, formative period of quantitative evolutionary biology's development. Dedicated his career to group selection theory (multi-level selection), an idea long dismissed as scientifically unimportant before contributing to its rigorous revival, with research funding from the National Science Foundation; studied multi-level selection in beetles. Taught organismal biology, evolution, biostatistics, philosophy of science, and conservation biology at UVM for decades. Real, direct personal significance: one of Nate Hagens's own real PhD committee members/advisors; Nate's own words describe 'long conversations with him in his beetle lab about animal behavior.' Real, close professional and personal collaborator of David Sloan Wilson, who called him directly 'a great friend and colleague' upon learning of his death. Per his own real UVM obituary: remembered for 'kind nature and optimism, good humor, infectious laugh, and strong sense of community and fellowship.'"@en ;
    .

tgs:Human.LisiKrall a thinkr:Human, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "Lisi Krall"@en ;
    rdfs:label "Lisi Krall (human)"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Professor of Economics, SUNY Cortland. Fulbright Scholar; SUNY Senior Scholar; recipient of the Chancellor's Award for Research. Real, long-standing academic collaborator of John Gowdy (already a real, built Persona in this graph via TGS_14) — their real, sustained co-authorship on ultrasociality and the economic Superorganism, including tgs:Work.Ultrasocial, predates this episode by years. Author of tgs:Work.BitterHarvest."@en ;
    .

tgs:Human.HermanDaly a thinkr:Human, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "Herman Daly"@en ;
    rdfs:label "Herman Daly (human)"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Professor Emeritus of Economics, University of Maryland School of Public Policy; former Senior Economist, World Bank; a founder of the field of ecological economics. Studied under Nicholas Georgescu-Roegen at Vanderbilt University, where exposure to Georgescu-Roegen's entropy-law economics (then in progress toward his own magnum opus) directly reshaped Daly's thinking. Author of tgs:Work.ForTheCommonGood (with John B. Cobb Jr.) and tgs:Work.ValuingTheEarth, and the textbook Ecological Economics (co-authored with Joshua Farley, already a real, built Persona in this graph). Real, extraordinary direct personal significance to Nate Hagens: Nate's own words describe reading Valuing the Earth as the main cause of his leaving his Wall Street career roughly a year later; when Nate personally asked to become Daly's PhD student, Daly — unable to take students at the time due to his own departmental standing at LSU — personally connected him instead to his colleague Josh Farley, which is how Nate ended up earning his PhD at the University of Vermont."@en ;
    .

tgs:Human.TomChi a thinkr:Human, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "Tom Chi"@en ;
    rdfs:label "Tom Chi (human)"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Founding partner, At One Ventures (backs early-stage deep-tech companies aiming to upend established industries' unit economics while dramatically reducing their planetary footprint). Previously a founding member of Google X, where he led teams that created self-driving cars, deep learning AI, wearable augmented reality (Google Glass), and internet-connectivity expansion projects. Earlier career spanned astrophysics research, Fortune 500 consulting, and corporate executive roles including Microsoft Outlook and Yahoo Search. Trained as a physicist. Real, distinctive contribution to this graph's own vocabulary: a real, structured three-epoch framework (material productivity, then a fuller 'net positive to nature' economy) for what it would take for the economy to become net positive to the biosphere rather than merely less negative."@en ;
    .

tgs:Human.NicholasGeorgescuRoegen a thinkr:Human, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "Nicholas Georgescu-Roegen"@en ;
    rdfs:label "Nicholas Georgescu-Roegen (human)"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Romanian-American mathematician, statistician, and economist (1906-1994). Best known for his 1971 magnum opus, The Entropy Law and the Economic Process, arguing that all natural resources are irreversibly degraded when used in economic activity — a foundational work establishing ecological economics as its own academic sub-discipline. Studied under Joseph Schumpeter at Harvard. Real, direct personal significance to this graph: Herman Daly's own real PhD advisor at Vanderbilt University, credited by Daly as the direct origin of his entire intellectual shift toward ecological economics. Real, notable relationship dynamic: Georgescu-Roegen later became a real, direct critic of Daly's own steady-state economy concept, arguing it could only postpone, not prevent, eventual mineral resource exhaustion. Widely regarded by the degrowth movement as its main real theoretical source."@en ;
    .

tgs:Human.ReinerKummel a thinkr:Human, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "Reiner Kümmel"@en ;
    rdfs:label "Reiner Kümmel (human)"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "German physicist (b. 1939), specializing in solid-state physics, thermodynamics, and econophysics. Professor of theoretical physics, University of Würzburg (1974-2004). Real, direct professional connection to Robert Ayres, whose later real work applying Kümmel's own methods (the LINEX production function) to US economic data over the 20th century is directly cross-cited elsewhere in this graph. His real, central contribution: demonstrating that when 'useful work' from energy is properly counted as its own factor of production alongside capital and labor, the unexplained 'Solow Residual' in standard growth models — usually attributed to unexplained technological progress — largely disappears, showing energy's real economic weight is far larger than its cost share alone suggests. Author of The Second Law of Economics: Energy, Entropy, and the Origins of Wealth (Springer, 2011)."@en ;
    .

tgs:Human.KennethBoulding a thinkr:Human, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "Kenneth E. Boulding"@en ;
    rdfs:label "Kenneth E. Boulding (human)"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "English-born American economist, educator, peace activist, and interdisciplinary philosopher (1910-1993). Co-founder of general systems theory. President, American Economic Association (1968-69). A devout Quaker and pacifist; helped organize the first Vietnam War teach-in at the University of Michigan in 1965. Real, direct significance to this graph: his influential 1966 essay, 'The Economics of the Coming Spaceship Earth,' argued the economic system must fit itself to the ecological system's real, limited resource pools — a real, direct precursor to the biophysical-economics framing this graph's own core concepts build on. Author of some 30 books and over 800 articles, including The Image: Knowledge in Life and Society (1956) and Conflict and Defense: A General Theory (1962), both real citation classics in their fields."@en ;
    .

tgs:Human.PaulShepard a thinkr:Human, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "Paul Shepard"@en ;
    rdfs:label "Paul Shepard (human)"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "American environmentalist and author (1925-1996). Best known for introducing the real 'Pleistocene paradigm' to deep ecology — the argument that human nature is a consciousness shaped by evolutionary and environmental conditions that predate agriculture, and that humans remain, in a real biological sense, 'beings of the Paleolithic.' Taught at Pitzer College and Claremont Graduate University (1973-1994). Real, direct significance to this graph: a foundational influence on Lisi Krall's own thinking about the agricultural revolution's role in shaping the human-nature duality her own real TGS appearance (TGS_86) explores directly. Notable books include The Tender Carnivore and the Sacred Game and Nature and Madness."@en ;
    .

tgs:Human.WesJackson a thinkr:Human, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "Wes Jackson"@en ;
    rdfs:label "Wes Jackson (human)"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "American geneticist (b. 1936). Co-founder, The Land Institute (1976), developing perennial grain, pulse, and oilseed polycultures as a real, direct alternative to conventional annual-grain agriculture's soil erosion and ecological costs. 1992 MacArthur Fellow; 2000 Right Livelihood Award laureate. Real, long-documented friendship and correspondence with Wendell Berry (already a real, built Persona in this graph), with whom he co-authored 'Meeting the Expectations of the Land.' Real, direct significance to this graph: a foundational influence, alongside Paul Shepard, on Lisi Krall's own thinking about the agricultural revolution (TGS_86). Author of Becoming Native to This Place and, more recently, An Inconvenient Apocalypse: Environmental Collapse, Climate Crisis, and the Fate of Humanity (2022, with Robert Jensen)."@en ;
    .

tgs:Human.WilliamRuddiman a thinkr:Human, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "William Ruddiman"@en ;
    rdfs:label "William Ruddiman (human)"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "American paleoclimatologist; Professor Emeritus, University of Virginia. PhD, marine geology, Columbia University (1969); worked at Columbia's Lamont-Doherty Earth Observatory (1976-1991). Best known for the real 'early anthropocene hypothesis' (the 'Ruddiman hypothesis'): that human-caused greenhouse gas increases began roughly 8,000 years ago with the intensive farming of early agrarian societies, not with 18th-century industrialization — and that this early human activity likely forestalled an incipient ice age that would otherwise have already begun. Also known for the real, earlier hypothesis (with then-student Maureen Raymo) that Himalayan/Tibetan Plateau tectonic uplift drove Cenozoic cooling through increased chemical weathering. Awarded the Lyell Medal, Geological Society of London (2010). Author of Plows, Plagues, and Petroleum and Earth Transformed."@en ;
    .

tgs:Human.LewisMumford a thinkr:Human, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "Lewis Mumford"@en ;
    rdfs:label "Lewis Mumford (human)"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "American historian, sociologist, philosopher of technology, and literary critic (1895-1990). Architectural critic for The New Yorker for over 30 years. Author of tgs:Work.TheMythOfTheMachine (two volumes, 1967 and 1970), which develops his real concept of the 'megamachine' — large hierarchical organizations using humans as their components, exemplified across history from pyramid-building to modern nuclear-armed states — and distinguishes 'polytechnic' (technology serving diverse human needs) from 'monotechnic' (technology pursued for its own sake, at humanity's expense). His earlier book, The City in History, won the 1962 National Book Award. Real, direct influence on a wide later intellectual lineage including E.F. Schumacher, Murray Bookchin, and Amory Lovins."@en ;
    .

tgs:Human.LutherKrueger a thinkr:Human, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "Luther Krueger"@en ;
    rdfs:label "Luther Krueger (human)"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Since 2004, has collected classic and contemporary solar cookers, promoting solar cooking as a means to halt deforestation, provide safe drinking water in remote areas of developing countries, and reduce fossil fuel dependence. Founder, the unincorporated, volunteer-run Big Blue Sun Museum of Solar Cooking; contributing moderator, Solar Cookers World Network. Senior Community Faculty member, Metropolitan State University, teaching the Capstone course for the Master of Public and Nonprofit Administration degree program. Retired from the Minneapolis Police Department in 2023 after 28 years as a civilian community liaison and crime analyst, where he developed and launched several community policing initiatives."@en ;
    .

tgs:Human.GarrettHardin a thinkr:Human, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "Garrett Hardin"@en ;
    rdfs:label "Garrett Hardin (human)"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "American ecologist and philosopher (1915-2003). Best known for his real, influential 1968 Science essay, 'The Tragedy of the Commons,' arguing shared, unregulated resources tend toward overexploitation absent some form of restraint or governance. Coined 'ecolate' (tgs:Concept.Ecolate) — ecological literacy as a third literacy alongside being numerate and literate — centered on habitually asking 'and then what?' of any proposed action or policy to trace its wider consequences."@en ;
    .

tgs:Human.IlyaPrigogine a thinkr:Human, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "Ilya Prigogine"@en ;
    rdfs:label "Ilya Prigogine (human)"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Belgian physical chemist (1917-2003), born in Russia. Won the 1977 Nobel Prize in Chemistry for his real work on dissipative structures, complexity, and irreversibility — showing that systems far from thermodynamic equilibrium can spontaneously self-organize into new, higher-order structures. Real, direct significance to this graph: his own real concept, often paraphrased directly by Nate — that small 'islands of coherence' within a system far from equilibrium have the capacity to shift the entire system to a higher order — is cited independently across multiple already-built episodes in this graph (Frankly_65, Frankly_98, Lisi Krall's TGS_86) as a recurring intellectual anchor."@en ;
    .

tgs:Human.MarvinHarris a thinkr:Human, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "Marvin Harris"@en ;
    rdfs:label "Marvin Harris (human)"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "American anthropologist (1927-2001). Developed 'cultural materialism,' a real, structured framework for analyzing societies as a three-level hierarchy: infrastructure (energy, environment, economy — the material foundation), structure (economic and political institutions, laws, regulations), and superstructure (ideas, beliefs, and values), with infrastructure treated as the primary driver of cultural change. Real, direct significance to this graph: cited independently across multiple already-built episodes (Simon Michaux's TGS_49, Frankly_98) as a recurring analytical framework."@en ;
    .

tgs:Human.OveHoeghGuldberg a thinkr:Human, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "Ove Hoegh-Guldberg"@en ;
    rdfs:label "Ove Hoegh-Guldberg (human)"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Professor of Marine Studies, University of Queensland, Brisbane; Deputy Director, Centre for Excellence in Coral Reef Studies; for the prior 10 years, Founding Director, Global Change Institute. Conceived and led the XL-Catlin Seaview Survey, which surveyed over 1,000 km of coral reefs across 25 countries and captured and analyzed over 1 million survey images. One of the most-cited climate scientists. One of the first scientists to identify climate change as a serious threat to coral reefs, in a real, landmark 1999 paper predicting coral reef loss by 2050."@en ;
    .

tgs:Human.RomanKrznaric a thinkr:Human, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "Roman Krznaric"@en ;
    rdfs:label "Roman Krznaric (human)"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Social philosopher. Senior Research Fellow, Oxford University's Centre for Eudaimonia and Human Flourishing; founder, the world's first Empathy Museum; member, Club of Rome; Research Fellow, Long Now Foundation. PhD, political science, University of Essex; also studied at Oxford and London. Grew up in Sydney and Hong Kong. Previously worked as a gardener, a conservation activist, and on human rights issues in Guatemala. A top-ranked player of the medieval sport of real tennis. Author of internationally bestselling books including The Good Ancestor, Empathy, Carpe Diem Regained, and tgs:Work.HistoryForTomorrow. Real, direct personal connection to this graph: husband of Kate Raworth, already a real, built Persona and repeat TGS guest."@en ;
    .

tgs:Human.PaulEhrlich a thinkr:Human, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "Paul Ehrlich"@en ;
    rdfs:label "Paul Ehrlich (human)"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Bing Professor Emeritus of Population Studies, Stanford University; retired conservation biologist. Author of tgs:Work.ThePopulationBomb (1968) and many other books, including The Race Bomb (with Shirley Feldman). Real, famous for over 20 real appearances on The Tonight Show Starring Johnny Carson discussing population — more than any other scientist. Born 1932; was approaching 90 at the time of this graph's real interview with him."@en ;
    .

tgs:Human.MartinScheringer a thinkr:Human, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "Martin Scheringer"@en ;
    rdfs:label "Martin Scheringer (human)"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Professor of Environmental Chemistry, Masaryk University, Brno, Czech Republic; Senior Scientist, ETH Zürich, in the research program on Environmental Chemistry and Modeling at RECETOX. Diploma in chemistry, Johannes-Gutenberg University, Mainz; doctoral degree and habilitation, ETH Zürich. Specializes in environmental and human exposure assessment, with real, direct involvement in science-policy debate on PFAS ('forever chemicals') and broader chemical pollution."@en ;
    .

tgs:Human.AudreyTang a thinkr:Human, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "Audrey Tang"@en ;
    rdfs:label "Audrey Tang (human)"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Taiwan's Cyber Ambassador-at-Large; Taiwan's first Digital Minister (2016-2024), where they led radical government-transparency initiatives making government data and resources publicly accessible. Named one of TIME's '100 Most Influential People in AI' (2023). Instrumental in shaping Taiwan's internationally-acclaimed COVID-19 response and in safeguarding the 2024 presidential and legislative elections from foreign cyber interference. Now focused on 'Plurality' — technology for collaborative diversity. Uses they/them pronouns."@en ;
    .

tgs:Human.JonathanHaidt a thinkr:Human, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "Jonathan Haidt"@en ;
    rdfs:label "Jonathan Haidt (human)"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Social psychologist, NYU Stern School of Business. PhD, University of Pennsylvania, 1992. Research examines the intuitive foundations of morality and how morality varies across cultural and political divisions. Author of The Happiness Hypothesis (2006), tgs:Work.TheRighteousMind (2012), and The Coddling of the American Mind (2018, with Greg Lukianoff). Inducted into the American Academy of Arts and Sciences, 2019. Since 2018, studies social media's contribution to declining teen mental health and rising political dysfunction; author of The Anxious Generation and, at the time of this episode, was writing 'Kids in Space' and 'Life After Babel.'"@en ;
    .

tgs:Human.PeterStrack a thinkr:Human, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "Peter Strack"@en ;
    rdfs:label "Peter Strack (human)"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Worked for 40 years in industrial research and engineering at MAHR France. After retiring, became aware of environmental crises and energy constraints, and went on to research, study, and advocate for 2000-watt neighborhoods. Author of tgs:Work.PratiquementDurable."@en ;
    .

tgs:Human.AlexisZeigler a thinkr:Human, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "Alexis Zeigler"@en ;
    rdfs:label "Alexis Zeigler (human)"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Self-taught activist, builder, mechanic, writer, and orchardist. Organized numerous political, environmental, and economic localization campaigns. Since 2010, has worked to build and grow Living Energy Farm, a zero-fossil-fuel, mostly self-sufficient farm prioritizing collective living principles, aiming to make sustainable technologies accessible regardless of income. Author of 'Empowering Communities.'"@en ;
    .

tgs:Human.AnnaLembke a thinkr:Human, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "Anna Lembke"@en ;
    rdfs:label "Anna Lembke (human)"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Professor of Psychiatry, Stanford University School of Medicine; Chief, Stanford Addiction Medicine Dual Diagnosis Clinic. Published over 100 peer-reviewed papers, book chapters, and commentaries. Sits on the board of several state and national addiction-focused organizations; has testified before US House and Senate committees. Author of 'Drug Dealer, MD' (2016, highlighted by the New York Times as a top book on the opioid epidemic) and tgs:Work.DopamineNation. Appeared in the Netflix documentary The Social Dilemma."@en ;
    .

tgs:Human.VictorGorshkov a thinkr:Human, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "Victor Gorshkov"@en ;
    rdfs:label "Victor Gorshkov (human)"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Russian theoretical physicist (deceased). Co-developed the real biotic pump theory (tgs:Concept.BioticPump) and the broader 'biotic regulation of the environment' framework alongside Anastassia Makarieva, arguing intact natural ecosystems actively regulate climate stability rather than passively responding to it. Real, direct significance credited by Makarieva herself: established a real, formal size-dependent limit on individual organisms' resource consumption share within stable ecosystems — larger organisms, having a lower surface-to-volume ratio, are structurally suppressed from disproportionate environmental impact in genuinely stable systems."@en ;
    .

tgs:Human.AnastassiaMakarieva a thinkr:Human, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "Anastassia Makarieva"@en ;
    rdfs:label "Anastassia Makarieva (human)"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Russian atmospheric physicist; Senior Researcher, Petersburg Nuclear Physics Institute. PhD, atmospheric physics, St. Petersburg State University, 2000. Co-developed the biotic pump theory (tgs:Concept.BioticPump) with the late Victor Gorshkov (already a real, built Human in this graph). Challenges conventional climate models to incorporate vegetation's own dynamic role in atmospheric processes rather than treating it as a passive background feature. Real, close personal friend and collaborator, since 2004, of Antonio Nobre (already built) — per her own real, direct account, Antonio was the one who wrote to her and Gorshkov that same year urging them to apply their biotic-regulation theory specifically to the Amazon's water cycle, directly prompting their subsequent 'flying rivers' research into the region."@en ;
    .

tgs:Human.JohanRockstrom a thinkr:Human, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "Johan Rockström"@en ;
    rdfs:label "Johan Rockström (human)"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Internationally recognized scientist on global sustainability; led development of the Planetary Boundaries framework (tgs:Concept.PlanetaryBoundaries). Leading scientist on global water resources, with over 25 years of applied water research in tropical regions and more than 150 research publications. Advisor for sustainable development at the World Economic Forum, UN SDSN, and UNFCCC conferences. Chair, advisory board, EAT Foundation; member, Earth League; chair, Earth Commission. Affiliated with the Potsdam Institute."@en ;
    .

tgs:Human.CarlosNobre a thinkr:Human, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "Carlos Nobre"@en ;
    rdfs:label "Carlos Nobre (human)"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Brazilian Earth scientist; Senior Researcher, University of São Paulo. PhD, Meteorology, MIT. Co-chair, Science Panel for the Amazon (SPA). Formerly chaired the Large-Scale Biosphere-Atmosphere Experiment in Amazonia (LBA). Foreign member, US National Academy of Sciences and the Royal Society; member, Brazilian Academy of Sciences and the World Academy of Sciences. Awarded the Volvo Environmental Prize and the AAAS Science Diplomacy Award, among others. Developer of tgs:Work.Amazonia4."@en ;
    .

tgs:Human.HelenaNorbergHodge a thinkr:Human, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "Helena Norberg-Hodge"@en ;
    rdfs:label "Helena Norberg-Hodge (human)"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Linguist, author, and filmmaker. Founder and Director, Local Futures (international non-profit); pioneer of the new economy movement; convener, World Localization Day. Studied linguistics at the University of London and with Noam Chomsky at MIT. Author of tgs:Work.AncientFutures and producer of the real, award-winning documentary 'The Economics of Happiness.' Co-founded the International Forum on Globalization and the Global Ecovillage Network; has helped initiate localization movements across every continent, including South Korea and Japan. Her real, nearly 50-year body of work has received support from figures including Jane Goodall, the Dalai Lama, Prince Charles, and Indira Gandhi."@en ;
    .

tgs:Human.PaulKingsnorth a thinkr:Human, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "Paul Kingsnorth"@en ;
    rdfs:label "Paul Kingsnorth (human)"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "English writer and poet. Co-founder, the Dark Mountain Project. Author of tgs:Work.AgainstTheMachine."@en ;
    .

tgs:Human.DanielQuinn a thinkr:Human, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "Daniel Quinn"@en ;
    rdfs:label "Daniel Quinn (human)"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "American author (1935-2018). Best known for tgs:Work.Ishmael (1992), which introduced the real, distinctive 'Takers and Leavers' framework distinguishing civilizations that believe the world was made for humans to master from cultures that understand humans as one part of a larger living community. Real, direct significance to this graph: Charles Eisenstein's own first major book, The Ascent of Humanity, was written in part as a direct response to Quinn's anti-civilization discourse."@en ;
    .

tgs:Human.CharlesEisenstein a thinkr:Human, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "Charles Eisenstein"@en ;
    rdfs:label "Charles Eisenstein (human)"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "American author, public speaker, and teacher. Work focuses on civilization, consciousness, money, and human cultural evolution, centered on his own real, coined concept of the 'story of separation' — the largely unspoken belief that each person is an isolated self, competing against other selves and against a nature made of indifferent, exploitable resources. BA, Mathematics and Philosophy, Yale University, 1989; spent the following decade as a Chinese-English translator before turning to writing and speaking full-time. Former speechwriter for Robert F. Kennedy Jr. Author of tgs:Work.TheAscentOfHumanity, tgs:Work.SacredEconomics, tgs:Work.TheMoreBeautifulWorld, and tgs:Work.ClimateANewStory. His most recent book at the time of this graph's own build, The Coronation, examined the COVID-19 pandemic as a mirror of civilization's relationship to control, fear, and uncertainty."@en ;
    .

tgs:Human.RobinWallKimmerer a thinkr:Human, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "Robin Wall Kimmerer"@en ;
    rdfs:label "Robin Wall Kimmerer (human)"@en ;
    owl:sameAs <https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Robin_Wall_Kimmerer> ;
    rdfs:comment "Potawatomi botanist, author, and Director, Center for Native Peoples and the Environment, SUNY College of Environmental Science and Forestry (SUNY-ESF). Enrolled citizen, Citizen Potawatomi Nation. BS, botany, SUNY-ESF; MS and PhD, plant ecology, University of Wisconsin-Madison. Proponent of Traditional Ecological Knowledge (TEK) as a real, distinct 'way of knowing,' and of 'two-eyed seeing' — deliberately combining Western scientific and Indigenous ways of understanding nature, drawn in part from her own family history (her paternal grandfather was sent to the Carlisle Indian Industrial School, a real assimilationist boarding school). Author of Gathering Moss (2003, John Burroughs Medal), tgs:Work.BraidingSweetgrass, and The Serviceberry (2024). 2022 MacArthur Fellow; named to the 2025 TIME 100. Real, direct significance to this graph: cited independently across at least three already-built episodes (Frankly_113, Jason Bradford's TGS_211, Pella Thiel's TGS_121) though she has never been a guest on the show."@en ;
    .

tgs:Human.AntonioNobre a thinkr:Human, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "Antonio Donato Nobre"@en ;
    rdfs:label "Antonio Donato Nobre (human)"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Brazilian Earth scientist, trained originally as an agronomist. Scientific Director, the Biotic Pump Greening Group. Born and raised in São Paulo; rebelled against Green Revolution industrial-agriculture orthodoxy during his own agricultural university studies before turning to Amazon ecology. His own real master's research found that much of the Amazon forest's structural wealth lies above ground rather than in its soil, including the real, striking discovery of ancient sand dunes buried beneath the rainforest in its northwestern reaches. Real, direct significance to this graph: the real, direct originator of applying Makarieva and Gorshkov's biotic regulation theory specifically to the Amazon and its water cycle — per Makarieva's own real, direct account, he wrote to them in 2004 urging them to show how the Amazon actively regulates its own water cycle, prompting their real, subsequent 'flying rivers' and biotic pump research into the region. Real, close friend and collaborator of Anastassia Makarieva (already built) since that same year, 2004. Real, notable family detail, per Makarieva's own account: younger brother of Carlos Nobre (already built in this graph), with the two brothers holding directly opposing views on the biotic pump — Carlos a skeptic from the theory's earliest days, Antonio one of its earliest and most direct proponents."@en ;
    .

tgs:Human.EliezerYudkowsky a thinkr:Human, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "Eliezer Yudkowsky"@en ;
    rdfs:label "Eliezer Yudkowsky (human)"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "American AI safety researcher and writer. Co-founder, Machine Intelligence Research Institute (MIRI). Real, early and influential voice on AI alignment risk, writing on the subject since before it entered mainstream discourse. Co-author, with Nate Soares (already built), of tgs:Work.IfAnyoneBuildsItEveryoneDies. Real, direct significance to this graph: credited alongside Soares with the 'grown, not crafted' framing for modern AI systems, directly discussed in Frankly_122 (already built) before this book itself was built here."@en ;
    .

tgs:Human.NateSoares a thinkr:Human, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "Nate Soares"@en ;
    rdfs:label "Nate Soares (human)"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "President, Machine Intelligence Research Institute (MIRI), where he sets the organization's vision and strategy. Working in AI alignment for over a decade; author of a large body of technical and semi-technical writing on value learning, decision theory, and power-seeking incentives in smarter-than-human AI. Prior to MIRI, worked as an engineer at Google and Microsoft, as a research associate at the National Institute of Standards and Technology, and as a contractor for the US Department of Defense. Co-author, with Eliezer Yudkowsky (already built), of tgs:Work.IfAnyoneBuildsItEveryoneDies."@en ;
    .

tgs:Human.AzaRaskin a thinkr:Human, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "Aza Raskin"@en ;
    rdfs:label "Aza Raskin (human)"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Co-founder, Earth Species Project (already built) and Center for Humane Technology (already built); co-host, Your Undivided Attention podcast. Trained as a mathematician and dark-matter physicist. Son of Jeff Raskin, the real, direct developer of the original Macintosh computer. Has taken three companies from founding to acquisition. Co-chairing member, World Economic Forum's Global AI Council; helped found Mozilla Labs. Named FastCompany's Master of Design; listed on Forbes' and Inc.'s 30-under-30."@en ;
    .

tgs:Human.PatrickKnodel a thinkr:Human, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "Patrick Knodel"@en ;
    rdfs:label "Patrick Knodel (human)"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "CEO, Knodel Foundation (already built), a German impact investing firm focused on sustainability and autonomy for the Global South. Also directs investments for purpose-driven startups through Pandion Innovation for Impact GmbH. Background in economics and entrepreneurship; his worldview shaped substantially through real, direct international travel."@en ;
    .

tgs:Human.JeffCurrie a thinkr:Human, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "Jeff Currie"@en ;
    rdfs:label "Jeff Currie (human)"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Chief Strategy Officer, Altis Partners. Previously Chief Strategy Officer of Energy Pathways at The Carlyle Group, where he currently serves as Senior Advisor. Former Global Head of Commodities Research, Goldman Sachs, where he spent nearly three decades and became one of Wall Street's leading commodity market strategists — known for advising clients through the real commodity 'supercycle' of the 2000s, the shale supply shock of the 2010s, and the twin shocks of the pandemic and the Russia-Ukraine war."@en ;
    .

tgs:Human.TomasBjorkman a thinkr:Human, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "Tomas Björkman"@en ;
    rdfs:label "Tomas Björkman (human)"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Swedish social entrepreneur and philosopher; trained originally in physics and mathematics before a career in investment banking. Founder, Ekskäret Foundation (already built); co-founder, the research institute Perspectiva (London), the Emerge media platform (Berlin), the 29k personal-development platform, and the Inner Development Goals (IDGs) framework. Member, Club of Rome; Fellow, Royal Swedish Academy of Engineering Science. Author of tgs:Work.TheMarketMyth, tgs:Work.TheNordicSecret (with Lene Rachel Andersen), and tgs:Work.TheWorldWeCreate. Divides his time between London, Stockholm, and Berlin."@en ;
    .

tgs:Human.StephenJenkinson a thinkr:Human, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "Stephen Jenkinson"@en ;
    rdfs:label "Stephen Jenkinson (human)"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Cultural activist and author. Co-founder, with his wife Nathalie Roy, of the Orphan Wisdom School (already built). Master's degree in theological studies, Harvard University; master's degree in social work, University of Toronto. Served for years as program director of a palliative care center in a major Toronto teaching hospital, providing counseling at hundreds of deathbeds. Author of tgs:Work.DieWise and tgs:Work.Matrimony."@en ;
    .

tgs:Human.ErnestBecker a thinkr:Human, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "Ernest Becker"@en ;
    rdfs:label "Ernest Becker (human)"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "American cultural anthropologist (1924-1974). Author of tgs:Work.DenialOfDeath (1973 Pulitzer Prize, awarded posthumously), The Birth and Death of Meaning, and Escape from Evil. Real, direct foundational influence on Terror Management Theory — Sheldon Solomon, Jeff Greenberg, and Tom Pyszczynski (already built or built alongside him) directly credit encountering Becker's work as young professors in the early 1980s as the real, direct origin of their own research program."@en ;
    .

tgs:Human.SheldonSolomon a thinkr:Human, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "Sheldon Solomon"@en ;
    rdfs:label "Sheldon Solomon (human)"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Professor of Psychology, Skidmore College. Co-developer of Terror Management Theory, alongside Jeff Greenberg and Tom Pyszczynski. Research supported by the National Science Foundation and the Ernest Becker Foundation; featured in the award-winning documentary Flight from Death: The Quest for Immortality. Author of tgs:Work.TheWormAtTheCore and tgs:Work.InTheWakeOf911. American Psychological Society Fellow; recipient of an American Psychological Association Presidential Citation (2007) and a Lifetime Career Award from the International Society for Self and Identity (2009)."@en ;
    .

tgs:Human.JoslinFaithKehdy a thinkr:Human, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "Joslin Faith Kehdy"@en ;
    rdfs:label "Joslin Faith Kehdy (human)"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Founder and Director, Recycle Lebanon (already built), working toward circular-economy system change since 2015. Lives off-grid, homesteading in the rural village of Baskinta, Lebanon."@en ;
    .

tgs:Human.JohnKitzhaber a thinkr:Human, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "John Kitzhaber"@en ;
    rdfs:label "John Kitzhaber (human)"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Over 40 years of experience in health care and health policy in both public and private sectors. Practiced as an emergency room physician for 15 years; served 14 years in the Oregon Legislature; served three terms as Governor of Oregon. Author of the Oregon Health Plan, through which hundreds of thousands of low- and moderate-income Oregon families gained access to health care. During his third term as Governor, chief architect of Oregon's Coordinated Care Organizations, the first statewide US effort built around the Triple Aim (better health, better quality, lower cost) with a focus on community and population health."@en ;
    .

tgs:Human.AndreGuimaraes a thinkr:Human, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "André Guimarães"@en ;
    rdfs:label "André Guimarães (human)"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Agronomist and economist. Executive Director, Amazon Environmental Research Institute (IPAM, already built). Facilitator, the Coalition on Climate, Forests and Agriculture, a real network of 200+ entities from the rural sector, academia, and civil society developing a long-term land-use vision for Brazil. Chairman of the Board, Amazon Institute for Mankind and the Environment. Founded and served as CEO of Brazil Forests, a company focused on forest management, conservation, and restoration."@en ;
    .

tgs:Human.ThomasCrowther a thinkr:Human, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "Thomas Crowther"@en ;
    rdfs:label "Thomas Crowther (human)"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Ecologist studying the connections between biodiversity and climate change. Chair, advisory council, United Nations Decade on Ecosystem Restoration. Founder, Restor (already built). Former professor, Department of Environmental Systems Science, ETH Zürich, where he started Crowther Lab, an interdisciplinary group studying how global-scale ecological systems interact to regulate climate. Named a World Economic Forum Young Global Leader (2021) for his work on biodiversity protection and restoration."@en ;
    .

tgs:Human.PeterTurchin a thinkr:Human, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "Peter Turchin"@en ;
    rdfs:label "Peter Turchin (human)"@en ;
    owl:sameAs <https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Peter_Turchin> ;
    rdfs:comment "Russian-American scientist. Founder of cliodynamics, applying quantitative, data-driven methods to the study of historical dynamics. Developed structural-demographic theory and the real, widely-cited concept of 'elite overproduction' — more aspirants for elite positions than a society can actually accommodate, a real driver of political instability. Director, the Seshat Databank project. Author of ten books, including War and Peace and War, Ages of Discord, and tgs:Work.EndTimes. CORRECTED 2026-09-02: an earlier pass here wrongly assumed he had never been a TGS guest — he has a real, direct solo appearance, TGS #164, already built in this graph."@en ;
    .

tgs:Human.GreggHenriques a thinkr:Human, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "Gregg Henriques"@en ;
    rdfs:label "Gregg Henriques (human)"@en ;
    owl:sameAs <https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gregg_Henriques> ;
    rdfs:comment "Professor of Psychology, James Madison University. Creator of the Tree of Knowledge (ToK) System, a real, integrative metatheory attempting to unify psychology as a coherent science by mapping the real relationships between matter, life, mind, and culture."@en ;
    .

tgs:Human.ChristopherMastropietro a thinkr:Human, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "Christopher Mastropietro"@en ;
    rdfs:label "Christopher Mastropietro (human)"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Trained philosopher. Co-author, with John Vervaeke (already built) and Filip Miscevic, of Zombies in Western Culture: A Twenty-First Century Crisis. Has worked directly with Vervaeke on adapting his own real Awakening from the Meaning Crisis lecture series into book form."@en ;
    .

tgs:Human.RobertLustig a thinkr:Human, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "Robert Lustig"@en ;
    rdfs:label "Robert Lustig (human)"@en ;
    owl:sameAs <https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Robert_Lustig> ;
    rdfs:comment "Pediatric endocrinologist; Professor Emeritus, University of California, San Francisco. Real, prominent critic of dietary sugar and processed food, known for his real, widely-viewed 2009 lecture 'Sugar: The Bitter Truth.' Author of Fat Chance and Metabolical."@en ;
    .

tgs:Human.LiseVanSusteren a thinkr:Human, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "Lise Van Susteren"@en ;
    rdfs:label "Lise Van Susteren (human)"@en ;
    owl:sameAs <https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lise_Van_Susteren> ;
    rdfs:comment "Forensic psychiatrist. Real, direct co-founder of the Climate Psychiatry Alliance, working on the psychological effects of climate change, including her own coined concept of 'pretraumatic stress.' Real, direct personal detail: a childhood neighbor of Nate Hagens's."@en ;
    .

tgs:Human.TyroneHayes a thinkr:Human, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "Tyrone Hayes"@en ;
    rdfs:label "Tyrone Hayes (human)"@en ;
    owl:sameAs <https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tyrone_Hayes> ;
    rdfs:comment "Professor of Integrative Biology, UC Berkeley. Real, direct research found the herbicide atrazine to be a potent endocrine disruptor in frogs, causing real, documented sexual abnormalities. His findings led to a real, extensively publicized, years-long conflict with Syngenta, the herbicide's manufacturer, who conducted a real, documented campaign to discredit him personally."@en ;
    .

tgs:Human.DavidKorten a thinkr:Human, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "David Korten"@en ;
    rdfs:label "David Korten (human)"@en ;
    owl:sameAs <https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/David_Korten> ;
    rdfs:comment "Author and prominent critic of corporate globalization. Co-founder, YES! Magazine. Author of the real, influential When Corporations Rule the World and The Great Turning: From Empire to Earth Community."@en ;
    .

tgs:Human.DavidSteindlRast a thinkr:Human, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "David Steindl-Rast"@en ;
    rdfs:label "David Steindl-Rast (human)"@en ;
    owl:sameAs <https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/David_Steindl-Rast> ;
    rdfs:comment "Austrian-American Benedictine monk. Real, direct teacher of gratefulness as a spiritual practice; co-founder, gratefulness.org (A Network for Grateful Living). Prominent figure in real interfaith dialogue between Christianity and Buddhism."@en ;
    .

tgs:Human.HazelHenderson a thinkr:Human, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "Hazel Henderson"@en ;
    rdfs:label "Hazel Henderson (human)"@en ;
    owl:sameAs <https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hazel_Henderson> ;
    rdfs:comment "British-American futurist and economist (1933-2022). Real, prominent critic of conventional GDP-based economics; pioneer of alternative economic and quality-of-life indicators. Founder, Ethical Markets Media. Author of Creating Alternative Futures and Building a Win-Win World."@en ;
    .

tgs:Human.PierLuigiLuisi a thinkr:Human, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "Pier Luigi Luisi"@en ;
    rdfs:label "Pier Luigi Luisi (human)"@en ;
    owl:sameAs <https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pier_Luigi_Luisi> ;
    rdfs:comment "Italian biochemist. Real, direct research on the origin of life, autopoiesis, and synthetic biology, particularly minimal self-replicating cell-like systems. Real, direct collaborator with Fritjof Capra on the systems view of life."@en ;
    .

tgs:Human.BarbaraMcClintock a thinkr:Human, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "Barbara McClintock"@en ;
    rdfs:label "Barbara McClintock (human)"@en ;
    owl:sameAs <https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Barbara_McClintock> ;
    rdfs:comment "American scientist and cytogeneticist (1902-1992). Awarded the 1983 Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine, unshared, for her real discovery of transposons ('jumping genes') — genetic elements capable of changing position within a genome, a discovery decades ahead of its time in molecular biology. Worked for most of her career at Cold Spring Harbor Laboratory."@en ;
    .

tgs:Human.KarlineJanmaat a thinkr:Human, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "Karline Janmaat"@en ;
    rdfs:label "Karline Janmaat (human)"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Primatologist. Affiliated with the Max Planck Institute and the University of Amsterdam. Real, direct research on primate cognition, spatial memory, and foraging strategies, particularly in wild chimpanzees."@en ;
    .

tgs:Human.JenniferJacquet a thinkr:Human, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "Jennifer Jacquet"@en ;
    rdfs:label "Jennifer Jacquet (human)"@en ;
    owl:sameAs <https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jennifer_Jacquet> ;
    rdfs:comment "Associate Professor of Environmental Studies, New York University. Real, direct research on shame as a collective-action mechanism for addressing large-scale environmental and animal-ethics problems. Author of Is Shame Necessary?"@en ;
    .

tgs:Human.HagaiLevine a thinkr:Human, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "Hagai Levine"@en ;
    rdfs:label "Hagai Levine (human)"@en ;
    owl:sameAs <https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hagai_Levine> ;
    rdfs:comment "Israeli epidemiologist, Hebrew University of Jerusalem. Real, direct co-author, with Shanna Swan, of major real meta-analyses documenting a significant global decline in human sperm counts over recent decades."@en ;
    .

tgs:Human.JoshSchrei a thinkr:Human, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "Joshua Michael Schrei"@en ;
    rdfs:label "Joshua Michael Schrei (human)"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Podcaster, mythteller, and teacher, focused on the cosmologies and mythologies of the world, particularly the Indian subcontinent. Founder, The Emerald Podcast (The Mythic Body), combining narrative, music, and interviews to explore myth and imagination. Has taught mythology and somatic disciplines for over 20 years."@en ;
    .

tgs:Human.RandyHayes a thinkr:Human, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "Randy Hayes"@en ;
    rdfs:label "Randy Hayes (human)"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Founder, Rainforest Action Network (RAN), a real, prominent organization campaigning against tropical deforestation since the 1980s. More recently involved with Foundation Earth and real rewilding advocacy work. Real, direct personal detail: named by Nate Hagens as 'my friend' during a real citation in another episode, though never himself a TGS guest."@en ;
    .

tgs:Human.PeteMyers a thinkr:Human, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "Pete Myers"@en ;
    rdfs:label "Pete Myers (human)"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Founder and Chief Scientist, Environmental Health Sciences, publisher of EHN.org and DailyClimate.org. Real, direct research background in endocrine-disrupting chemicals and their real, documented health effects."@en ;
    .

tgs:Human.AtossaSoltani a thinkr:Human, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "Atossa Soltani"@en ;
    rdfs:label "Atossa Soltani (human)"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Founder, Amazon Watch, a real, prominent organization protecting the Amazon rainforest and defending indigenous peoples' rights, working directly with indigenous communities across the Amazon basin including in Ecuador. Real, direct affiliations with the World Economic Forum, XPRIZE, and the Rights of Nature Tribunal."@en ;
    .

tgs:Human.JaneGoodall a thinkr:Human, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "Jane Goodall"@en ;
    rdfs:label "Jane Goodall (human)"@en ;
    owl:sameAs <https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jane_Goodall> ;
    rdfs:comment "English primatologist and anthropologist (1934-2025). Widely considered the world's foremost expert on chimpanzees, following her real, groundbreaking field research beginning at Gombe, Tanzania, in 1960. Founder, the Jane Goodall Institute; UN Messenger of Peace. Real, direct honors included the Kyoto Prize, the Templeton Prize, and the US Presidential Medal of Freedom (2025). Died October 1, 2025, at age 91."@en ;
    .

tgs:Human.DianFossey a thinkr:Human, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "Dian Fossey"@en ;
    rdfs:label "Dian Fossey (human)"@en ;
    owl:sameAs <https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dian_Fossey> ;
    rdfs:comment "American zoologist and primatologist (1932-1985). Conducted real, extensive field study of mountain gorilla populations in Rwanda; founded the Karisoke Research Center. Author of Gorillas in the Mist. Murdered in 1985 at her own research camp, a real, still-unsolved case widely believed connected to her real, direct anti-poaching activism."@en ;
    .

tgs:Human.ThichNhatHanh a thinkr:Human, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "Thích Nhất Hạnh"@en ;
    rdfs:label "Thích Nhất Hạnh (human)"@en ;
    owl:sameAs <https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Th%C3%ADch_Nh%E1%BA%A5t_H%E1%BA%A1nh> ;
    rdfs:comment "Vietnamese Thiền Buddhist monk, peace activist, and teacher (1926-2022). Real, widely credited with popularizing mindfulness practice in the West. Founder, Plum Village Tradition and Monastery in France. Nominated for the Nobel Peace Prize by Martin Luther King Jr."@en ;
    .

tgs:Human.LynnMargulis a thinkr:Human, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "Lynn Margulis"@en ;
    rdfs:label "Lynn Margulis (human)"@en ;
    owl:sameAs <https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lynn_Margulis> ;
    rdfs:comment "American evolutionary biologist (1938-2011). Real, direct originator of the serial endosymbiotic theory — that mitochondria and chloroplasts originated as free-living bacteria later incorporated into other cells — now the mainstream account of eukaryotic cell evolution. Real, direct collaborator with James Lovelock on developing the Gaia hypothesis. Elected to the National Academy of Sciences."@en ;
    .

tgs:Human.SamHarris a thinkr:Human, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "Sam Harris"@en ;
    rdfs:label "Sam Harris (human)"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Author of five New York Times bestsellers, including The End of Faith (2005 PEN Award for Nonfiction), The Moral Landscape, Free Will, Lying, tgs:Work.WakingUp, and Islam and the Future of Tolerance. Hosts the Making Sense podcast (Webby Award, Science & Education). BA, Philosophy, Stanford University; PhD, Neuroscience, UCLA. Has practiced meditation for over 30 years, studying with Tibetan, Indian, Burmese, and Western teachers. Creator, the Waking Up meditation app."@en ;
    .

tgs:Human.ThomasMurphy a thinkr:Human, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "Thomas Murphy"@en ;
    rdfs:label "Thomas Murphy (human)"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Professor, Physics Department, UC San Diego; Associate Director, Center for Astrophysics and Space Sciences (CASS). Author of tgs:Work.EnergyAndHumanAmbitions and the real, long-running 'Do the Math' blog. From 2003-2020, led the APOLLO project, an ultra-precise test of General Relativity using lunar laser ranging. His research interests have since shifted toward quantitative assessment of the challenges facing long-term human success on a finite planet."@en ;
    .
